\alias{LearnerRegrStochtreeBCF}
\title{Stochastic Tree Ensemble Bayesian Causal Forest Regression Learner}
\description{
Bayesian causal forest (BCF) sampled with MCMC,
optionally warm-started with the grow-from-root algorithm of accelerated BCF (XBCF).
Calls \code{stochtree::bcf()} from \CRANpkg{stochtree}.

The model decomposes the outcome into a prognostic forest and a treatment effect forest,
i.e., \code{y = mu(x) + tau(x) * z}, where \code{z} is the treatment.
As mlr3 has no task type for causal inference,
the treatment is a feature of the regression task that is selected with the \code{treatment} parameter.
The learner predicts the outcome given the covariates and the observed treatment,
so resampling and tuning evaluate the outcome prediction and not the treatment effect.
The estimated conditional average treatment effect is returned by the \verb{$treatment_effect()} method.

The \code{"se"} predict type returns the posterior standard deviation of the conditional mean.
Propensity scores are estimated internally by \code{stochtree::bcf()}.
Random effects are not supported.
}
\section{Custom mlr3 parameters}{

\itemize{
\item \code{treatment}: Name of the feature that contains the treatment.
The feature must be logical, integer, or numeric, and is passed as \code{Z_train} to \code{stochtree::bcf()}.
All other features are used as covariates.
This parameter is required.
\item \code{stochtree::bcf()} takes most of its parameters in the lists \code{general_params}, \code{prognostic_forest_params},
\code{treatment_effect_forest_params}, and \code{variance_forest_params}.
The entries of these lists are exposed as individual hyperparameters.
The entries of \code{general_params} keep their names.
The entries of the forest lists that occur in more than one list are prefixed with \code{prognostic_}, \code{treatment_},
and \code{variance_}, e.g., \code{prognostic_num_trees}, \code{treatment_num_trees}, and \code{variance_num_trees}.
All other entries of the forest lists keep their names.
\item \code{random_seed}: If not set, the seed of the C++ random number generator is drawn from R's random number generator.
Upstream seeds from \code{std::random_device} in this case.
}
}

\section{Reproducibility}{

\code{stochtree::bcf()} estimates the propensity scores with an internal BART model that is always seeded from
\code{std::random_device}.
Results are therefore only reproducible with \code{set.seed()} or \code{random_seed} when \code{propensity_covariate} is \code{"none"},
in which case no propensity model is fitted.
\itemize{
\item \code{observation_weights} is not exposed as a hyperparameter.
Observation weights are taken from the task instead, see the \code{weights} property.
}
}

